>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `Excl`

``` hol4
simpLib.Excl : string -> thm
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Excludes named simplifier components for one invocation.

`Excl name` is a directive in the theorem list of a simplification
conversion or tactic. A bare name uses the same parser and rule matching
as `ss -* [name]`: qualification uses dots, not the kernel `Thy$Name`
spelling. A two-part dotted name whose second part is not numeric
requires its theory part to be an ancestor of the current theory;
otherwise it raises `bad theory name`. An unmatched bare name is a silent
no-op. Thus bare `Excl "bool$AND_CLAUSES"` does not exclude the rewrite
`bool.AND_CLAUSES`.

If no rewrite or named decision procedure matches, a bare name can
instead exclude a looper, solver, split display or case display. Matching
several of these kinds is an ambiguity error. Use a namespace to select
the intended kind explicitly:

- `rule:name` excludes rewrites and named decision procedures. For
  rewrites it also accepts `Thy$Name` and theory parts that are not
  ancestors of the current theory.
- `looper:name` excludes an ordinary looper.
- `solver:name` excludes a named solver.
- `split:name` excludes the split rule with that name.
- `case:ty` excludes the datatype splits for `ty`.

Namespaced exclusions that match nothing raise an error. In particular,
use `Excl "case:bool"`, not the bare display name `"split.case bool"`,
whose dot invokes the strict rewrite-name parser.

### Failure

The constructor never fails. Processing the directive can fail for an
invalid bare name, an ambiguous target or an unmatched namespaced name.

### Example

``` hol4
>> SIMP_CONV bool_ss [Excl "bool$AND_CLAUSES"] “T ∧ p”;
>> QCONV (SIMP_CONV bool_ss [Excl "rule:bool$AND_CLAUSES"]) “T ∧ p”;
```

